53929 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353929 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353929 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353929 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186351718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353929 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "41d0c1c4-db7a-5402-823c-10bcfe18c23c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186351722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186351733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186353452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354222 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ece55d7d-c7d4-5233-ba8f-7f279a6be42b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354492 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${A}^{71},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18c9e3a0-322a-5b85-8d72-26e41db87e30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354492 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354495 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354495 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0ebddfd-78f1-5dda-8990-10394fcd7459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354495 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354496 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]T=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dc95dbe-f9a5-583f-bd3e-71e914c88035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354496 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186492090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491442 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3a218002-61a4-554f-ad7b-1fff0bdc3316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492080 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492080 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ba7f498-1881-5ab1-ad8c-dead16debd6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492080 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186354492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492090 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492090 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bdc3345-f3fe-56c7-9d48-45ed67552ad2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492090 #edb/